1
00:00:02,867 --> 00:00:05,026
Imagine a grower has a crop plant that

2
00:00:05,147 --> 00:00:07,546
produces exactly the fruit they want.

3
00:00:07,827 --> 00:00:10,306
Making new plants from it keeps that useful

4
00:00:10,427 --> 00:00:12,026
inherited combination.

5
00:00:12,387 --> 00:00:14,466
Now a new disease arrives.

6
00:00:14,787 --> 00:00:17,266
How could copying a productive plant become a

7
00:00:17,427 --> 00:00:18,626
shared risk?

8
00:00:18,667 --> 00:00:21,506
The answer begins with what reproduction passes from

9
00:00:21,587 --> 00:00:23,266
one generation to the next.

10
00:00:25,267 --> 00:00:28,546
An individual has a limited lifetime, yet its

11
00:00:28,707 --> 00:00:31,626
descendants may continue for many generations.

12
00:00:31,907 --> 00:00:34,586
That connected sequence is a lineage.

13
00:00:34,947 --> 00:00:37,986
Reproduction makes the link by producing offspring and

14
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:41,106
passing on hereditary information in DNA.

15
00:00:41,427 --> 00:00:44,706
In this schematic, each generation receives information from

16
00:00:44,747 --> 00:00:45,746
the previous one.

17
00:00:46,227 --> 00:00:49,346
The lifetimes are compressed; real parents can live

18
00:00:49,587 --> 00:00:50,866
alongside their offspring.

19
00:00:51,507 --> 00:00:54,706
Viable means able to live and develop; being

20
00:00:54,867 --> 00:00:57,066
fertile is a separate requirement.

21
00:00:57,347 --> 00:01:00,546
For a population to continue, enough descendants must

22
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:02,706
survive and reproduce in turn.

23
00:01:04,734 --> 00:01:08,093
Both methods pass on hereditary information, but they

24
00:01:08,134 --> 00:01:09,533
do it differently.

25
00:01:09,654 --> 00:01:14,253
Asexual reproduction produces offspring without gametes fusing, typically

26
00:01:14,334 --> 00:01:15,853
from one parent.

27
00:01:15,934 --> 00:01:20,573
Sexual reproduction involves gametes, reproductive cells, fusing during

28
00:01:20,654 --> 00:01:22,173
fertilisation.

29
00:01:22,254 --> 00:01:24,573
The cell formed is a zygote.

30
00:01:24,614 --> 00:01:27,293
There are usually two parents, but some organisms

31
00:01:27,414 --> 00:01:30,413
can self-fertilise using their own gametes.

32
00:01:30,494 --> 00:01:34,173
That is still sexual reproduction because gametes fuse.

33
00:01:34,654 --> 00:01:36,733
We will also use the word allele.

34
00:01:37,214 --> 00:01:39,693
An allele is a version of a gene;

35
00:01:39,774 --> 00:01:42,573
differences in inherited alleles are one form of

36
00:01:42,654 --> 00:01:43,933
genetic variation.

37
00:01:45,934 --> 00:01:48,093
A strawberry plant can grow a new plant

38
00:01:48,174 --> 00:01:50,613
on a runner without gametes fusing.

39
00:01:50,894 --> 00:01:54,573
The offspring usually retains very similar genetic information,

40
00:01:54,694 --> 00:01:56,573
so we describe it as a clone.

41
00:01:57,134 --> 00:01:59,773
When the parent performs well, this passes on

42
00:01:59,854 --> 00:02:02,493
a useful combination without needing a mate.

43
00:02:03,054 --> 00:02:06,813
Under suitable conditions, an asexual population can increase

44
00:02:06,894 --> 00:02:10,493
rapidly, depending on the organism and its resources.

45
00:02:10,974 --> 00:02:13,613
There are two different reasons clones can differ.

46
00:02:14,174 --> 00:02:17,293
Mutation can alter DNA in cells that contribute

47
00:02:17,374 --> 00:02:18,333
to offspring.

48
00:02:18,654 --> 00:02:21,453
Different light or water can change growth even

49
00:02:21,534 --> 00:02:23,853
with very similar DNA.

50
00:02:23,894 --> 00:02:26,333
A difference caused by those growing conditions does

51
00:02:26,414 --> 00:02:29,853
not, by itself, show a new inherited allele.

52
00:02:31,867 --> 00:02:36,386
Sexual reproduction gives inherited information another route forward.

53
00:02:36,667 --> 00:02:40,506
In outcrossing, gametes from different individuals fuse.

54
00:02:41,067 --> 00:02:45,226
Gamete formation and fertilisation can assemble different combinations

55
00:02:45,267 --> 00:02:47,226
of the alleles already available.

56
00:02:47,867 --> 00:02:51,306
Offspring from genetically different parents can therefore differ

57
00:02:51,387 --> 00:02:53,546
from each other and from their parents.

58
00:02:54,107 --> 00:02:55,786
It is like dealing a new hand from

59
00:02:55,827 --> 00:02:59,546
an existing set of cards: the combinations change,

60
00:02:59,627 --> 00:03:02,066
using alleles that are already there.

61
00:03:02,587 --> 00:03:05,066
Finding a mate and producing gametes can also

62
00:03:05,307 --> 00:03:06,586
take time and energy.

63
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:10,120
Return to the grower.

64
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:13,600
Cloning preserves the crop characteristics they value.

65
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:16,600
But if those clones share an inherited susceptibility

66
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:19,400
to a pathogen, many may be vulnerable to

67
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:20,600
the same threat.

68
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:24,360
A genetically varied population could contain combinations that

69
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:28,440
reduce susceptibility, if that relevant variation is present.

70
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:32,600
Infection also depends on exposure, pathogen behaviour and

71
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:36,440
growing conditions, so genetic susceptibility is only part

72
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:37,600
of the outcome.

73
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:40,360
Cloning can be useful when a genotype suits

74
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:44,280
the conditions; varied inherited combinations may help when

75
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:45,480
conditions change.

76
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:48,200
Neither method is a universal winner.

77
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:51,400
Here are three events.

78
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:54,600
A strawberry makes a runner: a new plant

79
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:57,400
grows without gamete fusion, so the event is

80
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:58,200
asexual.

81
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:02,360
Coral eggs and sperm fuse: that fertilisation is

82
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:03,240
sexual.

83
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:06,040
Now a plant uses sperm produced from its

84
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:08,360
own pollen to fertilise an egg.

85
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:10,840
There is only one parent plant, yet two

86
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:12,760
gametes still fuse.

87
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:14,440
The event is sexual.

88
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:17,080
Parent number is a useful clue, but gamete

89
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:19,160
fusion is the stronger reason.

90
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:21,320
The next lessons will unpack how these events

91
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:23,160
occur in different organisms.

92
00:04:25,167 --> 00:04:27,486
Suppose one clone becomes infected.

93
00:04:27,887 --> 00:04:29,806
Does that tell us every clone must be

94
00:04:29,887 --> 00:04:30,446
infected?

95
00:04:31,007 --> 00:04:33,966
The mistake is turning a shared susceptibility into

96
00:04:34,047 --> 00:04:35,246
a certain outcome.

97
00:04:35,727 --> 00:04:38,366
The plants may share a weakness, but one

98
00:04:38,487 --> 00:04:41,006
infected plant does not establish what will happen

99
00:04:41,127 --> 00:04:42,446
to every other plant.

100
00:04:44,467 --> 00:04:47,106
A nursery grows cuttings from one parent and

101
00:04:47,187 --> 00:04:50,666
seedlings from crosses among genetically different parents.

102
00:04:51,027 --> 00:04:54,146
Some inherited combinations reduce susceptibility to a new

103
00:04:54,227 --> 00:04:55,226
disease.

104
00:04:55,587 --> 00:04:57,586
Which group is likely to show a wider

105
00:04:57,747 --> 00:05:00,706
range of inherited susceptibility, and why does that

106
00:05:00,867 --> 00:05:03,426
not promise every plant will survive?

107
00:05:03,747 --> 00:05:05,906
Pause here to explain your reasoning.

108
00:05:13,934 --> 00:05:15,933
The seedlings are likely to show a wider

109
00:05:16,014 --> 00:05:19,613
range of inherited susceptibility because the crosses combine

110
00:05:19,774 --> 00:05:22,573
alleles from genetically different parents.

111
00:05:22,654 --> 00:05:25,373
The cuttings usually retain a much narrower set

112
00:05:25,454 --> 00:05:27,853
of combinations from one parent.

113
00:05:27,894 --> 00:05:31,693
In this case, some combinations reduce susceptibility, but

114
00:05:31,814 --> 00:05:34,893
particular seedlings may not inherit them.

115
00:05:35,014 --> 00:05:37,613
Even reduced susceptibility is not the same as

116
00:05:37,854 --> 00:05:40,813
complete protection, so neither group can be promised

117
00:05:41,054 --> 00:05:42,093
complete survival.

118
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:48,260
Reproduction connects generations by producing offspring and passing

119
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:49,460
on DNA.

120
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:53,860
Asexual reproduction usually retains a parent combination without

121
00:05:53,940 --> 00:05:55,340
gametes fusing.

122
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:59,700
Sexual reproduction fuses gametes and can reshuffle inherited

123
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:00,420
alleles.

124
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:02,980
A combination that works well in one set

125
00:06:03,020 --> 00:06:05,540
of conditions may become a weakness when those

126
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:06,820
conditions change.

127
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:09,700
Next we will look at how animals achieve

128
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:13,300
reproduction, followed by plants and other organisms.
